Step 2: Generating Excel Charts
Instead of using external plotting libraries, we embed a Line Chart directly into the First_Derivatives sheet. 
This provides an immediate visual of the rate of voltage change compared to time.

Python code for generating excel charts

# 5. Create the Excel Chart
chart = LineChart()
chart.title = "ECG First Derivative Analysis"
chart.style = 13
chart.y_axis.title = "mV/s"
chart.x_axis.title = "Time (s)"

# Reference the data in the First_Derivatives sheet
# Note: min_col=2 is the 'First Derivative' column; min_row=2 skips the header
data = Reference(ws_deriv, min_col=2, min_row=2, max_row=len(t)+1)
cats = Reference(ws_deriv, min_col=1, min_row=2, max_row=len(t)+1)

chart.add_data(data, titles_from_data=False)
chart.set_categories(cats)

# 6. Add chart to the sheet and save
ws_deriv.add_chart(chart, "E2") # Places chart at cell E2
wb.save("ECG_Analysis_Results.xlsx")
print("Workbook generated with embedded charts.")
